    61	The number of vulnerabilities reported in open source software has
    62	increased substantially in recent years. Security patches provide the
    63	necessary measures to protect software from attacks and vulnerabilities. In practice, it is difficult to identify whether patches have
    64	been integrated into software, especially if we only have binary
    65	files. Therefore, the ability to test whether a patch is applied to the
    66	target binary, a.k.a. patch presence test, is crucial for practitioners.
    67	However, it is challenging to obtain accurate semantic information
    68	from patches, which could lead to incorrect results.
    69	In this paper, we propose a new patch presence test framework
    70	named Ppt4J ( **P** atch **P** resence **T** est **for J** ava Binaries). Ppt4J is
    71	designed for open-source Java libraries. It takes Java binaries (i.e.
    72	bytecode files) as input, extracts semantic information from patches,
    73	and uses feature-based techniques to identify patch lines in the binaries. To evaluate the effectiveness of our proposed approach Ppt4J,
    74	we construct a dataset with binaries that include 110 vulnerabilities.
    75	
    76	The results show that Ppt4J achieves an F1 score of 98.5% with reasonable efficiency, improving the baseline by 14.2%. Furthermore,
    77	we conduct an in-the-wild evaluation of Ppt4J on JetBrains IntelliJ
    78	IDEA. The results suggest that a third-party library included in the
    79	software is not patched for two CVEs, and we have reported this
    80	potential security problem to the vendor.
