   122	**1** **INTRODUCTION**
   123	
   124	
   125	The reuse of open source libraries is widespread [ 25 ]. Vulnerabilities in open-source software have become a major concern, posing
   126	significant threats to software and users [ 17 ]. For example, the
   127	number of Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs [ 6 ]) reported during the first quarter of 2023 has already exceeded the
   128	total number of CVEs reported in 2016 [ 37 ]. Although upstream
   129	developers may discover and fix these vulnerabilities over time,
   130	vulnerable versions may still propagate to downstream software or
   131	libraries, potentially compromising the security of the systems that
   132	rely on them.
   133	Since open source libraries are frequently distributed as binary
   134	files, it is essential that developers and users in the software supply
   135	chain be aware of potential vulnerabilities in the libraries they
   136	introduce. For example, _Log4Shell_ (CVE-2021-44228 [ 33 ]) is a wellknown vulnerability in Apache Log4j [ 12 ] that can lead to the
   137	execution of arbitrary code. If a software development team has
   138	integrated Log4j into their project, developers should verify if their
   139	version of Log4j is vulnerable to _Log4Shell_ . In other words, they
   140	have to confirm whether the binary contains the patch for CVE
   141	2021-44228.
   142	
   143	The above process of testing whether a security patch is applied
   144	to the program binaries is called _patch presence test_ [ 44 ]. However,
   145	traditional approaches that only take binary files for analysis cannot
   146	be utilized for patch presence tests due to the coarse granularity [ 44 ].
   147	For example, binary bug search tools, such as Genius proposed
   148	by Feng et al. [ 10 ], identify vulnerability types but cannot test
   149	the presence of an arbitrary patch commit. Similarly, binary code
   150	search tools, such as Tracy proposed by David et al. [ 8 ], find similar
   151	functions but cannot tell whether the function is patched or not.
   152	To accurately test the presence of a patch in fine granularity,
   153	Zhang et al. propose FIBER [ 44 ], a patch presence test framework
   154	for C/C++ binaries that extracts a localized part of patch _diff_ [ 40 ] for
   155	
   156	
   157	ICSE ’24, April 14–20, 2024, Lisbon, Portugal Zhiyuan Pan, Xing Hu, Xin Xia, Xian Zhan, David Lo, and Xiaohu Yang
   158	
   159	
   160	
   161	signature generation. BScout [ 7 ], another framework that targets
   162	Java binaries, utilizes the entire patch _diff_ .
   163	However, existing studies on patch presence test for Java binaries
   164	still have the following two limitations:
   165	
   166	
   167	  - **Inability to capture minor changes.** The features extracted by current approaches are unable to handle some subtle modifications to the source code (e.g., changing method
   168	call parameters, branch conditions and statements outside
   169	method bodies).
   170	
   171	  - **Limited patch semantic.** The _diffs_ utilized in existing approaches cannot perfectly reflect semantic changes (i.e., actual discrepancies in program behavior) in the patches, leading to the inclusion of extraneous information that does not
   172	exist in the patches.
   173	
   174	
   175	Given the widespread application of Java, such as server-side programs and Android applications, the limitations mentioned above
   176	highlight the need for a more comprehensive and accurate approach.
   177	To address these limitations, we propose a new patch presence test
   178	framework named Ppt4J ( **P** atch **P** resence **T** est **for J** ava Binaries).
   179	Ppt4J exploits the correspondence between source code features
   180	and binary features. It first extracts features from the source code
   181	to generate semantic changes. Then, the semantic changes guide
   182	Ppt4J to perform source-to-binary feature matching and feature
   183	queries. Finally, Ppt4J provides the test result by summarizing the
   184	queries.
   185	To evaluate the effectiveness of Ppt4J, we construct a dataset
   186	with binaries that include 110 vulnerabilities in total. We compare
   187	Ppt4J with a state-of-the-art Java patch presence test framework,
   188	BScout [ 7 ], in terms of accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score. To
   189	evaluate the effectiveness of Ppt4J in real-world software, we perform an in-the-wild evaluation by testing the presence of patches
   190	in various versions of JetBrains IntelliJ IDEA [ 38 ]. The results on
   191	the dataset demonstrate that Ppt4J achieves an F1 score of 98.5%,
   192	improving the baseline by 14.2% while maintaining reasonable efficiency. The results of the in-the-wild evaluation indicate that Ppt4J
   193	remains accurate in real-world scenarios. In addition, our evaluation reveals that Ppt4J has the ability to uncover instances where
   194	application vendors (e.g., JetBrains) have failed to apply security
   195	patches to third-party libraries they import, further demonstrating
   196	its utility in practical settings.
   197	In summary, we make the following contributions:
   198	
   199	
   200	(1) We propose a novel framework for Java binaries, Ppt4J, to
   201	accurately test the presence of patches by highlighting semantic code differences from patches.
   202	(2) We construct a dataset to evaluate the effectiveness of Ppt4J
   203	by obtaining source code from GitHub repositories, labeling
   204	ground truths, and building binaries.
   205	(3) We evaluate Ppt4J using the dataset and real-world software. Ppt4J outperforms the baseline and is able to test the
   206	presence of patches in real-world scenarios.
   207	(4) We release the replication package of Ppt4J [1], including the
   208	source code of Ppt4J and our dataset, to facilitate future
   209	research.
