   227	
   228	**2** **BACKGROUND**
   229	
   230	
   231	In this section, we discuss the background of the patch presence
   232	test task and provide a motivating example to illustrate the main
   233	idea of Ppt4J.
   234	
   235	
   236	**2.1** **Patch Presence Test**
   237	
   238	
   239	The task of patch presence test and its scope of the problem are
   240	defined as follows:
   241	
   242	
   243	_Definition._ Given a security patch _diff_ _𝑃_ from a specific open
   244	source library in the upstream, patch presence test works by evaluating target program binaries _𝐵_ on a boolean function
   245	
   246	
   247	_𝑓_ : ( _𝐶_ 1 _,𝐶_ 2 _, 𝑃, 𝐵_ ) →{True _,_ False} _,_
   248	
   249	
   250	where _𝐶_ 1 and _𝐶_ 2 refer to the upstream source code of the software
   251	right before and after patch _𝑃_ . _𝐵_ refer to the standard Java bytecode
   252	in this paper and should be provided by the user. Among _𝐶_ 1 _,𝐶_ 2
   253	and _𝑃_, at least two of them must be provided and the third can be
   254	automatically derived.
   255	
   256	
   257	Patch presence test checks if a specific patch is applied to the
   258	provided target binaries [ 44 ]. In this task, the upstream source code
   259	_𝐶_ 1 _,𝐶_ 2 and the target binaries _𝐵_ are supposed to belong to the same
   260	library, but the target binaries can be compiled from any version
   261	of source code. The function _𝑓_ has two possible return values. If
   262	_𝑓_ returns true, we confirm the existence of a patch commit within
   263	the binaries, and vice versa.
   264	The advantage of patch presence test lies in its ability to detect
   265	specific patch commits. This enables users to specifically check for
   266	security patches that they are most concerned about. Thus, the
   267	potential risks that arise from vulnerabilities can be mitigated.
   268	
   269	
   270	**2.2** **Motivating Example**
   271	
   272	
   273	Existing patch presence test work for Java binaries takes the complete _diff_ (i.e., the exact differences in characters) as input to analyze
   274	patch presence. However, existing work is limited as the whole _diff_
   275	will introduce “semantic redundancy”, that is, some (-) and (+)
   276	lines end up with no semantic changes, which may introduce unrelated information.
   277	
   278	Intuitively, to accurately test the presence of a patch, we should
   279	extract precise semantic changes from _diff_ that reflect all semantic information while not including unrelated information. We illustrate the importance of semantic changes by using real-world
   280	examples shown in Figure 1.
   281	In the first case of Figure 1, the _diff_ introduces a try-catch
   282	block with six _diff_ lines. However, there are no differences between lines 1, 2, and 5, 6, except for indentations. If we trim the
   283	
